The moonlit street opens onto Old Well Square, a broad flagstone plaza where the town’s namesake well stands like a squat stone sentinel. Shutters rattle on the timbered houses that hem the square, and every doorway gapes black. A single lantern swings from the iron bracket outside the bakery, its flame guttering in the cold wind.

Callum paces beside the well, boots scuffing grit across the stones. His patched vest flaps as he turns, eyes wide under a salt-and-pepper beard.

“Hey—you there,” he calls, voice low and hoarse. “My cart got hit last night. They took the mule, the barrels, everything. But the bastards dropped a little lockbox—my trade samples—somewhere along the Whispering Forest Edge. I’d fetch it myself, but…” He glances toward the eastern road where the trees knit into darkness. “I’ll pay what I can. Just bring it back.”

